1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,506
Everyone posts their NeurIPS acceptances.

2
00:00:02,506 --> 00:00:04,706
Nobody posts how many were rejected.

3
00:00:05,106 --> 00:00:11,821
Of the five top AI conferences, only ICLR
publishes its rejections. Since 2020,

4
00:00:11,821 --> 00:00:17,942
55 lecturers and researchers in Vietnam
sent 82 papers to ICLR in total;

5
00:00:17,942 --> 00:00:19,812
only 13 were accepted.

6
00:00:20,212 --> 00:00:25,102
This year they submitted to NeurIPS again,
and 32 papers were accepted.

7
00:00:25,102 --> 00:00:27,030
If the odds are like ICLR's,

8
00:00:27,030 --> 00:00:31,094
behind them are about 60 to 170
papers that did not get in.

9
00:00:31,494 --> 00:00:35,311
Of them, 22 have submitted but
have no accepted paper yet.

10
00:00:35,311 --> 00:00:38,207
And outside ICLR nobody
sees the rejections,

11
00:00:38,207 --> 00:00:40,906
so the real number of attempts is higher.

12
00:00:41,306 --> 00:00:44,458
And who writes the accepted papers?

13
00:00:44,458 --> 00:00:48,511
Of the 90 since 2020
with known affiliations,

14
00:00:48,511 --> 00:00:55,716
only 38 have a first author from the
lecturer's university and only 28 have most

15
00:00:55,716 --> 00:01:00,129
authors there,
while 40 have most authors abroad.

16
00:01:00,529 --> 00:01:03,915
A paper has 5.7 authors on average,
23 at most.

17
00:01:03,915 --> 00:01:07,948
One accepted paper is good
news for many people at once,

18
00:01:07,948 --> 00:01:11,118
so everyone on it can
say they have a paper.

19
00:01:11,518 --> 00:01:16,366
Rejections still outnumber acceptances,
even for people who already have papers.

20
00:01:16,366 --> 00:01:18,124
Students without a paper yet:

21
00:01:18,124 --> 00:01:19,821
don't be hard on yourselves.

22
00:01:19,821 --> 00:01:21,518
Full data at the link below.
